<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles the Simple</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/879">879</a>–<a href="/years/929">929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Charles III of France</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles the Simple was a Carolingian King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a>, crowned in 893 and sole king from 898. In 911, by the treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, he granted the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouen">Rouen</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a>, the origin of the Duchy of Normandy. He also gained Lotharingia. Rebel nobles deposed him in 922, and he died a prisoner.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-the-simple.jpg" alt="Charles the Simple" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: cgb.fr · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Denier_sous_Charles_III_le_Simple.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles was born on 17 September 879, the third and posthumous son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-stammerer">Louis the Stammerer</a> and his second wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide">Adelaide</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He was a child of five when his half-brother, King Carloman II, died on 6 December 884, and the Frankish nobles asked their cousin, Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-fat">Charles the Fat</a>, to take the crown instead. After Charles the Fat was deposed in 887, the nobility again passed over the young prince and elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-of-france">Odo, Count of Paris</a>, the hero of the siege of Paris of 885 to 886, as king. Charles was put under the protection of Ranulf II, Duke of Aquitaine.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 893, aged 14, Charles was crowned at Reims Cathedral by a faction opposed to Odo, aided by Archbishop Fulk of Reims. By 897 he ruled only the city of Laon. He became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-francia">West Francia</a> in 898, when Odo on his deathbed designated him as his successor. He soon showed ambition to conquer Lotharingia, the cradle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a>, and invaded in 898 at the call of Count Reginar of Hainaut.</p><p>In 911 a band of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rollo">Rollo</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a>. After a victory near Chartres on 26 August, Charles negotiated with Rollo, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-clair-sur-epte">Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte</a> created the Duchy of Normandy. The Vikings received the land between the river Epte and the sea, and Rollo agreed to be baptised and to marry Charles's daughter Gisela. Also in 911, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a>, the last Carolingian king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-francia">East Francia</a>, died, and the Lotharingian nobles under Reginar declared Charles their king, in preference to Conrad I, whom East Francia had elected. Charles defended Lotharingia against two attacks by Conrad.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles's favouritism towards Hagano, a petty nobleman and a relative of his first wife Frederuna, turned the aristocracy against him. He gave Hagano monasteries that were already the benefices of other barons. The nobles seized him in 920, and Archbishop Herveus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> negotiated his release. In 922 the nobles revolted again under Robert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a>, Odo's brother, who was crowned king, and Charles fled to Lotharingia. Returning in 923 with a Norman army, he was defeated by Robert's forces at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soissons">Soissons</a> on 15 June, and Rudolph of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, Robert's son-in-law, was elected king. Charles was captured by Herbert II of Vermandois and imprisoned, finally at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronne">Péronne</a>.</p><p>He died in prison on 7 October 929 and was buried in the monastery of Saint-Fursy. His son Louis by his second wife Eadgifu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> was crowned in 936 as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-of-france">Louis IV of France</a>, with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-the-great">Hugh the Great</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles the Simple” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Rollo’s failed attack on Chartres in 911, King <b>Charles the Simple</b> of West Francia granted him lands around Rouen in return for defending the Seine against other raiders and accepting baptism.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-normandy">Duchy of Normandy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 911 <b>Charles the Simple</b> granted the leader Rollo lands around Rouen, the origin of the Duchy of Normandy.</blockquote><a href="/culture/vikings">Vikings · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The failure led to the Treaty of Saint-Clair-sur-Epte, by which King <b>Charles the Simple</b> granted Rollo lands around Rouen.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-20">20 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King Robert I fell in battle against the deposed Carolingian <b>Charles the Simple</b>, though his side won the field.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-15">15 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles the Simple</b>, king of West Francia, and Henry the Fowler, king of East Francia, signed a &quot;pact of friendship&quot; aboard a ship in the middle of the Rhine near Bonn.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Herbert II was the most powerful lord of northern France in the 920s, holding <b>King Charles the Simple</b> prisoner at Péronne until the king's death in 929 and seizing the archbishopric of Reims for his young son.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-vermandois">County of Vermandois</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Péronne, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Péronne, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.93,2.93&amp;year=911" aria-label="Péronne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4617 426 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles the Simple"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5067 696h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5067 696h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.93,2.93&amp;year=911">What was here in 911 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
